/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - MISRA C:2012 sorts its guidelines as mandatory (never broken), required (broken only with a formal deviation) and advisory.
   - Passing MISRA gives disciplined code; it is not proof of functional safety.
   - ISO 26262:2018 has 12 parts and follows a V-model: requirements down the left side, implementation at the bottom, and verification and validation back up the right.
   - Each hazardous event gets a safety goal that inherits its ASIL, such as "no unintended full braking at speed".
   - The fault tolerant time interval (FTTI) is the minimum time from a fault to a possible hazardous event.
*/
