/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The vehicle carries an on-board unit (OBU), a radio with a small computer.
   - V2V, V2I and V2P can work directly, with no SIM or tower; only V2N needs cellular coverage.
   - d is the distance (m) at which a warning must reach the driver; vr is the closing speed (m/s); tw is the warning time the driver needs (s); Δt is the radio and processing latency (s).
   - Two radio families compete.
   - The telematics control unit (TCU) is the car's cellular gateway: a modem, a GNSS receiver for position and time, and an embedded SIM.
   - The head unit is the cabin computer behind the central screen.
   - Connection widens the attack surface: the cellular link, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, USB, the OTA server and the diagnostic socket.
   - A connected autonomous vehicle uses V2X as one more sensor beside radar (range and speed), camera (lanes, signs) and lidar (a 3-D point cloud).
*/
